---
import LegalLayout from "../layouts/LegalLayout.astro";
import { site, contact } from "../lib/site";

const toc = [
  { id: "agreement", label: "The agreement" },
  { id: "account", label: "Your workspace and seats" },
  { id: "acceptable-use", label: "Acceptable use" },
  { id: "whatsapp", label: "WhatsApp platform rules" },
  { id: "fees", label: "Fees, billing and changes" },
  { id: "data", label: "Who owns what" },
  { id: "availability", label: "Availability and support" },
  { id: "liability", label: "Warranties and liability" },
  { id: "termination", label: "Ending the agreement" },
  { id: "law", label: "Governing law and notices" },
] as const;

const summary = [
  "Pay per seat per month in USD. Cancel whenever you like; we do not lock you into a year.",
  "Your data stays yours, and you can take all of it with you on the way out.",
  "No bulk unsolicited messaging. It breaks WhatsApp's rules, gets your number banned, and we will not help you do it.",
  "We are pre-seed and say so: no formal uptime SLA below the Scale plan, and our liability is capped at what you paid us.",
  "If we change these terms in a way that matters, you get 30 days' notice and can walk away.",
] as const;
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Terms of Service"
  description="The agreement between your business and Repli: seats and billing in USD, acceptable use, WhatsApp platform rules, data ownership, and how either side ends it."
  kicker="Legal"
  heading="Terms of Service"
  lead="A short contract, written in the language we would use on the phone. No clause here is designed to be discovered late."
  updated="1 September 2026"
  toc={toc}
  summary={summary}
>
  <h2 id="agreement">1. The agreement</h2>
  <p>
    These terms are the agreement between {site.legalName} ("{site.name}", "we") — the Indonesian
    company that operates {site.name} — and the organisation that opens a workspace ("you"). They
    take effect when your workspace is created and apply for as long as it exists.
  </p>
  <p>
    If we have signed a separate order form or master agreement with you, that document wins wherever
    the two disagree. Our <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a> is part of these terms.
  </p>
  <p>
    Whoever accepts these terms must be authorised to bind your organisation. Because access to {site
      .name} starts with a call rather than a signup form, we will have established that before you
    have a workspace at all.
  </p>

  <h2 id="account">2. Your workspace and seats</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A seat is one person.</strong> Sharing one login across a team is not permitted — it
      destroys the audit trail, which is half of what you are paying for. Add and remove seats as your
      team changes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>You are responsible for your seats.</strong> Keep credentials secret, remove people who
      leave, and use the role controls we give you. Actions taken with valid credentials are treated
      as yours.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tell us about compromise quickly.</strong> If you think a login is compromised, write to
      <a href={`mailto:${contact.supportEmail}`}>{contact.supportEmail}</a> and we will help you lock
      it down.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>You must own the WhatsApp number you connect,</strong> or be authorised by whoever does.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Minimum age is 18,</strong> and the service is for business use, not personal use.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="acceptable-use">3. Acceptable use</h2>
  <p>You agree not to use {site.name} to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>send unsolicited bulk messages, or any message a recipient has opted out of receiving;</li>
    <li>run fraud, phishing, impersonation, or anything designed to deceive the person reading it;</li>
    <li>market goods or services that are illegal where your customer is;</li>
    <li>upload malware, or attempt to breach, probe or overload our infrastructure;</li>
    <li>reverse engineer the service, resell it, or rebuild it as a competing product;</li>
    <li>
      present AI-generated replies as human when a customer directly asks whether they are talking to
      a machine. Answer honestly; the product is built to let you.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We enforce this. If use is causing active harm — to your customers, to another workspace, or to
    the platform — we may suspend the offending capability immediately and explain afterwards. For
    anything less urgent you get notice and a chance to fix it first.
  </p>

  <h2 id="whatsapp">4. WhatsApp platform rules</h2>
  <p>
    {site.name} sits on top of the WhatsApp Business Platform, and Meta's policies apply to your
    messages whether or not you have read them. In practice that means:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Template messages need approval,</strong> and approval is Meta's decision, not ours. We
      will tell you why a template was rejected if we know.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Outside the 24-hour service window,</strong> you can only reach a customer with an
      approved template. This is a platform rule; no tool can route around it, and any vendor claiming
      otherwise is describing a policy violation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Quality rating and messaging limits are set by Meta</strong> based on how your
      recipients react. Bad practice lowers your rating and can get your number restricted or banned
      permanently.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Conversation-based charges are Meta's,</strong> not ours. Where they apply, they are
      billed by the platform separately from your {site.name} subscription.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If Meta suspends your number for something you did, we will help you understand it and appeal, but
    we cannot reverse it and we do not refund the subscription for that period.
  </p>

  <h2 id="fees">5. Fees, billing and changes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Priced per seat, per month, in USD.</strong> Annual billing is charged up front at the
      lower rate; monthly billing renews on the same day each month.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Adding a seat</strong> is prorated to the rest of the period. <strong
        >Removing a seat</strong
      > takes effect at the next renewal — we do not issue partial refunds for seats you stop using.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Voice minutes for the AI agent</strong> are the only usage-based item, billed monthly in
      arrears. Everything else is a fixed subscription. Meta's conversation charges reach you through
      Meta.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Taxes</strong> — Indonesian VAT and any withholding required by law are added or
      withheld as applicable.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Late payment.</strong> If an invoice is 14 days overdue we will chase you like humans
      first. After 30 days we may suspend the workspace. We do not delete data for non-payment inside
      the retention window described in the Privacy Policy.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Price changes</strong> get 30 days' written notice and never take effect mid-term.
      Design-partner and founding rates are locked for as long as the workspace stays active — that
      promise survives any general price rise.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="data">6. Who owns what</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Your data is yours.</strong> Contacts, conversations, media, notes, scores, reports —
    everything your team and your customers put in. You grant us only the licence we need to run the
    service for you, and it ends when the agreement does.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The software is ours.</strong> These terms give you the right to use {site.name}, not to
    own any part of it. Feedback you give us we may use freely to improve the product, without owing
    you anything for it — and without attaching your name to it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Export is unconditional.</strong> Full CSV and JSON export is available from the interface
    at any time, including while an invoice is disputed. We will never hold your data hostage to close
    a commercial argument.
  </p>

  <h2 id="availability">7. Availability and support</h2>
  <p>
    We aim for 99.9% monthly uptime and we publish incidents honestly rather than quietly. Being
    straight with you: <strong
      >a contractual uptime SLA with service credits is available on the Scale plan only.</strong
    > On Starter and Growth, that target is a commitment of effort, not a term you can enforce.
  </p>
  <p>
    Support runs {contact.hours}, with a first response typically {contact.responseTime.toLowerCase()}.
    Scale customers get a shared channel with a named engineer. Planned maintenance is announced at
    least 48 hours ahead and scheduled outside Indonesian business hours where we can.
  </p>
  <p>
    Parts of the service depend on third parties — WhatsApp, our AI provider, our cloud host. When one
    of them has an outage, we tell you what is broken and what we are doing. We do not take
    responsibility for their uptime, and no honest vendor would.
  </p>

  <h2 id="liability">8. Warranties and liability</h2>
  <p>
    We provide the service with reasonable skill and care. Beyond that, and to the extent the law
    allows, it is provided "as is": we do not warrant that it will be uninterrupted, error-free, or
    that AI output will always be correct.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>AI replies are drafts your business is responsible for.</strong> The agent can be wrong. Keep
    a human in the loop for anything that commits you — prices, legal statements, medical or financial
    advice — and use the escalation rules for exactly that purpose.
  </p>
  <p>
    Neither side is liable for indirect, incidental or consequential loss, or for lost profits, revenue
    or goodwill. Our total liability for any claim is capped at the fees you paid us in the
    <strong>12 months</strong> before the claim arose. Nothing here limits liability for death or
    personal injury caused by negligence, for fraud, or for anything else that cannot lawfully be
    limited.
  </p>
  <p>
    You indemnify us against claims arising from your content or your use of the service in breach of
    section 3 or 4 — most obviously, a claim from someone you messaged without permission.
  </p>

  <h2 id="termination">9. Ending the agreement</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>You can cancel at any time</strong> from the workspace or by emailing us. Monthly plans
      stop at the end of the current period. Annual plans run to the end of the term you paid for; we
      do not refund the unused part, and we will say so plainly before you choose annual.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>We can end it</strong> with 30 days' notice, or immediately if you materially breach
      these terms and do not fix it within 14 days of being told.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>If we ever shut down the product,</strong> you get at least 90 days' notice, a full
      export, and a pro-rata refund of anything you prepaid. We would rather write that down now than
      improvise it later.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>On termination:</strong> access ends, export stays available for 30 days, and we delete
      production data 60 days after cancellation as described in the
      <a href="/privacy#retention">Privacy Policy</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="law">10. Governing law and notices</h2>
  <p>
    These terms are governed by the laws of the Republic of Indonesia. Disputes go to the courts of
    South Jakarta — after a genuine attempt to sort it out on a call, which has worked every time so
    far.
  </p>
  <p>
    We may update these terms as the product changes. Material changes come with 30 days' notice by
    email to workspace admins and a new date at the top of this page; if a change is unacceptable to
    you, you may cancel before it takes effect and we will refund the unused prepaid portion.
  </p>
  <p>
    Notices to us go to <a href={`mailto:${contact.privacyEmail}`}>{contact.privacyEmail}</a> or by
    post to {site.legalName}, {contact.officeLines.join(", ")}. Notices to you go to your workspace
    admins. If a clause here turns out to be unenforceable, the rest stands.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
